# Everything is text, stored with LF, and CHECKED OUT with LF on every
# platform. `eol=lf` beats `core.autocrlf`, which is what made Windows
# checkouts CRLF: vitest rewrote snapshots with the checkout's endings on
# every `pnpm test` (a pure-EOL diff dirtying the tree), and staged edits
# became whole-file rewrites (a 9-line doc change staged as 761/584) —
# unreviewable under the required review flow.
#
# `text=auto` leaves binary-DETECTED files alone. Today that is exactly the
# three tests carrying literal NUL bytes (the key-separator and timing-safe
# fixtures); nothing else in the repo is binary, and those files are stored
# LF around their NULs, so nothing here would touch them anyway.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Vitest writes snapshots with the working tree's endings, so the pin is
# explicit rather than inherited: a snapshot that ever gains content that
# confuses the text heuristic must still round-trip as LF text.
**/__snapshots__/*.snap text eol=lf

# Scripts predate the blanket rule: a CRLF checkout made a module that
# opened with a shebang fail to parse on windows-latest ("SyntaxError:
# Invalid or unexpected token"). The shebang is gone; the pin stays
# explicit so the story survives the blanket rule above.
scripts/*.mjs text eol=lf
scripts/*.js  text eol=lf

# Future binary assets opt out of text handling entirely (= -text -diff
# -merge), rather than trusting the heuristic per file.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.gz   binary
*.zip  binary
